\section*{Quillen's \$p\$-Subgroup Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
For every finite group and prime with trivial p-core, the elementary-abelian p-subgroup poset is claimed to have nonzero augmented rational homology, proving a stronger homological form of Quillen's conjecture. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For a finite group $G$ and prime $p$, let $O_p(G)$ be its largest normal $p$-subgroup and $\mathcal A_p(G)$ the poset of nonidentity elementary abelian $p$-subgroups, ordered by inclusion. OpenAI claims
\[
O_p(G)=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\widetilde H_*(\mathcal A_p(G);\mathbb Q)\ne0.
\]
Homology is that of the order complex and uses augmented reduced chains, with $\widetilde H_{-1}(\varnothing;\mathbb Q)=\mathbb Q$. This is rational homological nonvanishing, which implies noncontractibility.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 310, 2026-09-24): Apartment chains from line frames are related by plane changes so that their boundaries cancel. Lie-superbracket encodings and a Poincare--Birkhoff--Witt estimate bound the surviving cycle space while retaining form types and automorphism constraints. These cycles meet the needed component-extension cases, and explicit full-chain propagation passes their nonzero coefficients into the ambient subgroup poset.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 310, 2026-09-24): Nonzero augmented reduced rational homology whenever O\_p(G)=1 excludes contractibility of the elementary-abelian-subgroup complex, hence proves the conjecture by contraposition for every finite group and prime, with the empty-poset convention included.

\subsection{Formal verification}
